  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout info">CROSS-REFERENCE: §§242–245 supply the GENERAL scaffolding for the annual accounts – WHAT must be prepared, to WHAT standard, in WHICH language and currency, and WHO must sign it. The specific RECOGNITION rules (§§246–251) and MEASUREMENT rules (§§252–256a) that fill in this scaffolding are covered in the next two posters.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">The bridge between bookkeeping and the finished annual accounts</div>
      <div class="principle">Where §§238–241a required the underlying BOOKS and INVENTORY to exist, §§242–245 require those raw materials to be turned INTO a finished, dated, signed set of annual accounts – prepared IN GERMAN, IN EUROS, and to a standard of GoB compliance and "clear insight" that the rest of Book 3 then elaborates in painstaking detail.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§242–245 MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Grundsätze ordnungsmäßiger Buchführung (GoB)</strong> – §243(1) explicitly requires the annual accounts to be prepared "unter Beachtung der Grundsätze ordnungsmäßiger Buchführung," carrying GoB compliance forward from bookkeeping into the finished statements themselves</span>
        <span><strong>Klarer Einblick (clear insight)</strong> – §243(2)'s own, HGB-specific formulation of the idea that financial statements should genuinely inform their readers – conceptually adjacent to, but structurally DIFFERENT from, IFRS's fair presentation override (Section 7)</span>
        <span><strong>Rechtssicherheit (legal certainty)</strong> – mandating German language, Euro currency, and personal signature removes ambiguity about WHAT was filed and WHO is accountable for it</span>
      </div>
      <div class="note">§242 applies the SAME basic obligation to every Kaufmann; §§264 et seq. (a later Titel) then layer ADDITIONAL requirements specifically onto Kapitalgesellschaften.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§242–245 require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>242</span><span>Prepare an opening balance sheet, then a Bilanz + GuV (= Jahresabschluss) each year-end</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>243</span><span>Prepare it per GoB, giving a clear insight, within a reasonable time</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>244</span><span>German language, Euro currency unit</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>245</span><span>Personally signed, and dated, by the Kaufmann(s)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout info">§242(4) carries the §241a small-business exemption FORWARD – a qualifying sole trader need not prepare a Bilanz or GuV at all, consistent with their exemption from §§238–241 in the prior Titel.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every Kaufmann not exempted under §241a</div>
      <div class="principle">§§242–245 bind every Kaufmann bound by §§238–241 in the preceding Titel – the SAME population, carried forward one step. Where §241a exempts a small sole trader from bookkeeping and inventory obligations, §242(4) mirrors that exemption for the annual accounts themselves, so the two Titel move together as a single, coherent unit rather than creating any gap.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§242 – the Obligation to Prepare (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">An opening balance sheet, then a Bilanz and a GuV every year thereafter</div>
      <div class="principle">§242(1) requires the Kaufmann to prepare, at the START of their commercial activity, an Eröffnungsbilanz (opening balance sheet) presenting the relationship between their assets and their debts – and, for the CLOSE of every subsequent business year, a further such statement (simply called the Bilanz). §242(2) additionally requires, for the close of each business year, a comparison of the year's expenses and income – the Gewinn- und Verlustrechnung (GuV). §242(3) states plainly: the Bilanz and the GuV TOGETHER form the Jahresabschluss (annual accounts).</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: Identifying what a new business must prepare, and when</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A newly-founded GmbH begins trading on 1 April. Its first full fiscal year runs to 31 December of the SAME year (a short first year, as is common for newly-founded companies).</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The GmbH must prepare an Eröffnungsbilanz as of 1 April (its founding date) under §242(1), AND, separately, a full Jahresabschluss (Bilanz + GuV) as of 31 December for its first – short – business year, per §242(1)–(3). Being a Kapitalgesellschaft, it is bound by §242 REGARDLESS of its size, since §242(4)'s exemption applies ONLY to Einzelkaufleute meeting the §241a thresholds.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Bilanz, GuV, and the Jahresabschluss – How the Pieces Fit</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Two statements, one combined term</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">"Jahresabschluss" is NOT a third, separate document – it is simply the COLLECTIVE name §242(3) gives to the Bilanz and GuV TOGETHER. For a Kapitalgesellschaft, the Jahresabschluss is later EXPANDED by §264(1) to also include a mandatory Anhang (notes), making the notes a third, equally mandatory component specifically for corporations – though NOT for an ordinary Einzelkaufmann or partnership outside §264a's scope.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cw">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Where the Lagebericht fits</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The Lagebericht (management report), required separately for Kapitalgesellschaften under §264(1) and detailed later in §289, is DELIBERATELY kept OUTSIDE the Jahresabschluss definition – it accompanies the Jahresabschluss but is not part of it, a structural distinction with real legal consequences for audit scope and adoption procedures.</div>
      <div class="callout warn">Do not conflate "Jahresabschluss" (Bilanz + GuV, plus Anhang for corporations) with the BROADER set of documents a Kapitalgesellschaft ultimately files – which also includes the Lagebericht as a separate, accompanying report.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§243(1) – Preparation in Accordance with GoB</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Carrying the bookkeeping standard forward into the finished statements</div>
      <div class="principle">§243(1) requires the Jahresabschluss ITSELF to be prepared in accordance with GoB – this is not redundant with §238's own GoB reference for the underlying books, since a Jahresabschluss could, in principle, be internally consistent with the books yet still fail GoB at the level of PRESENTATION, AGGREGATION, or CLASSIFICATION choices made in constructing the final statements from those books.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>§243(2) – the "Klarer Einblick" (Clear Insight) Requirement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The annual accounts, insofar as general provisions permit, must give a clear insight</div>
      <div class="principle">§243(2) states that the Jahresabschluss must give a CLEAR INSIGHT (klaren Einblick) into the net assets and results of operations of the enterprise, "SOWEIT NACH DEN VORAUSGEGANGENEN VORSCHRIFTEN MÖGLICH IST" – that is, TO THE EXTENT the preceding general provisions PERMIT. This qualifying phrase is doing significant legal work – it is discussed in Section 7.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Klarer Einblick vs IFRS's Fair Presentation Override</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A principle that supplements specific rules, rather than overriding them</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">The phrase "soweit... möglich ist" is the crux of the difference. Under IAS 1.19–20, IFRS permits an entity, in EXTREMELY rare circumstances, to actually DEPART from a specific requirement of a Standard where management concludes compliance would be so misleading it conflicts with the Framework's objective – a genuine, if rarely used, OVERRIDE mechanism. §243(2)'s "clear insight" requirement, by contrast, operates WITHIN the boundaries the specific individual provisions of Book 3 already set – it does NOT license departure from a specific recognition or measurement rule merely because the aggregate picture might otherwise seem clearer. Where the specific rules genuinely fail to convey a fair picture for a Kapitalgesellschaft, the LEGAL remedy is not departure from those rules but ADDITIONAL DISCLOSURE in the Anhang (a mechanism made explicit later, in §264(2) – see the Related Provisions map, Section 18).</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB §243(2)</span><span>IFRS (IAS 1.19–20)</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Response to a misleading picture</span><span>ADDITIONAL disclosure in the Anhang (via §264(2), for corporations)</span><span>Genuine DEPARTURE from a specific IFRS requirement, in extremely rare cases</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Relationship to specific line-item rules</span><span>Operates WITHIN them – "soweit... möglich ist"</span><span>Can, exceptionally, OVERRIDE them</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">This is a genuinely important, easily-missed distinction for anyone approaching HGB with IFRS instincts already formed – reaching for a "fair override" the way IAS 1 permits is simply NOT available under HGB's own clear-insight requirement.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§243(3) – Timely Preparation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A general standard, later made specific for corporations</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§243(3) requires the Jahresabschluss to be prepared within a period "entsprechend den Grundsätzen ordnungsmäßiger Geschäftsführung" – a period consistent with orderly business management – a DELIBERATELY general, flexible standard at this point in Book 3. A later, more specific Titel (§264(1), not yet covered in this poster series) converts this general standard into concrete deadlines for Kapitalgesellschaften – generally three months after the fiscal year-end for larger corporations, extendable to six months for smaller ones.</div>
      <div class="note">The GENERAL §243(3) standard remains the operative rule for Einzelkaufleute and ordinary partnerships, who are NOT subject to §264's specific corporate deadlines.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Handelsbilanz vs Steuerbilanz – the Maßgeblichkeitsprinzip in Action</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">The commercial balance sheet as the authoritative starting point for tax</div>
      <div class="principle">§243 is the provision commonly annotated, in German commercial-law commentary, with the relationship between the Handelsbilanz (the commercial balance sheet §242–243 requires) and the Steuerbilanz (the tax balance sheet used to compute taxable income). Under the Maßgeblichkeitsprinzip (§5(1) EStG, the Income Tax Act – outside HGB itself, but directly anchored to it), the COMMERCIAL balance sheet is, subject to specific tax-law adjustments, AUTHORITATIVE for determining the TAX balance sheet – a linkage with NO parallel in an IFRS-only jurisdiction, where financial and tax accounting typically run as entirely SEPARATE computations from the outset.</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: Tracing a single balance sheet figure into two different outcomes</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH recognises a €120,000 provision (Rückstellung) for an onerous long-term contract in its Handelsbilanz, fully compliant with §249 (a later Titel). German tax law, however, imposes its OWN, narrower conditions on the deductibility of certain provisions, permitting only €90,000 of this amount to be recognised for tax purposes.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The Handelsbilanz correctly reflects €120,000, GOVERNED entirely by HGB's own recognition rules. The Steuerbilanz starts from that SAME €120,000 figure (Maßgeblichkeit) but then makes a SPECIFIC tax-law adjustment REDUCING the deductible amount to €90,000 – illustrating both the authoritative STARTING role the commercial balance sheet plays, and the fact that tax law can and does DEPART from it through its own express provisions.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying the Clear Insight Requirement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Choosing additional disclosure over departing from a specific rule</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A misleading picture that specific rules alone cannot fix</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A Kapitalgesellschaft's specific balance sheet line items, prepared strictly per Book 3's individual recognition and measurement rules, technically comply with every applicable provision – but a major, imminent legal risk with severe potential financial consequences is not adequately visible anywhere in the Bilanz or GuV themselves, since no SPECIFIC line item exists to capture it at this stage.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The company does <strong>NOT</strong> depart from any specific HGB measurement or recognition rule to force the risk onto the face of the Bilanz. Instead, ADDITIONAL disclosure describing the risk is added to the Anhang (per the mechanism §264(2) makes explicit for corporations) – preserving the specific rules' integrity while still delivering the "clear insight" §243(2) demands, achieved through DISCLOSURE rather than departure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>§244 – Language and Currency Unit (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">German, and Euros – with no exception for foreign parents</div>
      <div class="principle">§244 requires the Jahresabschluss to be prepared in GERMAN and in EUROS – a simple provision on its face, but one that creates genuine practical friction for foreign-owned German subsidiaries.</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A US-parented German subsidiary's dual reporting burden</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A German GmbH, wholly owned by a US parent, reports monthly management information to its US parent in English and US Dollars, consolidated under US GAAP. At its German statutory year-end, however, it must ALSO prepare a fully separate set of HGB annual accounts.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">REGARDLESS of its US parent's own reporting language and currency, the GmbH's HGB Jahresabschluss must be prepared entirely in <strong>GERMAN</strong> and <strong>EUROS</strong> – §244 permits NO exception for foreign ownership. The GmbH therefore maintains, in effect, two parallel sets of financial information: its own HGB-compliant German/EUR statutory accounts, and whatever reporting package its US parent separately requires for consolidation purposes.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§245 – Signature (with worked example)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Personal accountability, made explicit on the document itself</div>
      <div class="principle">§245 requires the Jahresabschluss to be signed by the Kaufmann, WITH the date of signing – WHERE there are several personally liable partners, ALL of them must sign. This is a formal, individual-accountability requirement with no true IFRS analogue – IFRS financial statements are typically approved through a board resolution process governed by company law, rather than through an HGB-specific, individually-mandated personal signature provision.</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 5: Signature requirements for a multi-partner OHG</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An OHG (general partnership) has three personally liable partners. At year-end, the annual accounts are finalised and ready for signature.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">ALL THREE partners must sign the Jahresabschluss, each with the date – the signature obligation is NOT satisfied by a single managing partner signing on behalf of the others; §245 requires each personally liable partner's OWN signature.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Worked Example – a Missing Signature From One Partner</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A refusal to sign does not invalidate the accounts, but must be documented</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 6: A dissenting partner refuses to sign</div>
        <div class="wk-body">Continuing the OHG example above, one of the three partners disputes the valuation of a major asset and refuses to sign the annual accounts, despite the other two partners being satisfied that the accounts are properly prepared.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The REMAINING two partners sign the Jahresabschluss, and the refusal – together with its stated reason – is NOTED alongside the signatures. The dissenting partner's refusal does NOT, by itself, prevent the accounts from being validly prepared and signed by those partners willing to do so, though it may carry SEPARATE legal consequences for the partnership's internal governance and any subsequent dispute.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Practitioners should treat the specific procedural consequences of a partner's refusal with care – the underlying partnership agreement and general partnership law (not §245 alone) will often govern what happens next.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Disclosure &amp; Formal Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">What §§242–245 collectively require to appear on the finished document</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>An opening balance sheet at commencement, and a Bilanz + GuV (Jahresabschluss) for each subsequent fiscal year-end</span>
        <span>Preparation consistent with GoB, giving a clear insight within the boundaries the specific provisions permit</span>
        <span>Preparation within a period consistent with orderly business conduct (or the specific §264(1) deadline, for corporations)</span>
        <span>The German language and the Euro currency unit, throughout</span>
        <span>Personal signature, with date, by the Kaufmann or, where several, by ALL personally liable partners</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether the specific line items of the Bilanz/GuV, taken together, genuinely convey a clear insight, or whether supplementary Anhang disclosure is required</span>
            <span>What period is "consistent with orderly business conduct" for an Einzelkaufmann or partnership not bound by §264's specific deadlines</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Maintaining parallel German/EUR statutory accounts alongside a foreign parent's own group reporting language and currency</span>
            <span>Securing timely signature from all personally liable partners in a partnership experiencing internal disagreement</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – HGB §§242–245 vs IFRS</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB (§§242–245)</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Fair-presentation-style requirement</span><span>Klarer Einblick (§243(2)) – supplemented via disclosure, not overridden</span><span>Fair presentation (IAS 1) – with a rare, genuine override mechanism</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Mandated language and currency</span><span>German and EUR, by statute, for HGB accounts</span><span>NO equivalent – IFRS is agnostic to language and presentation currency, left to local law</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Personal signature requirement</span><span>Explicit, individual, statutory (§245)</span><span>NO direct equivalent – approval is a company-law/governance matter, not an IFRS requirement</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">§§242–245 illustrate a RECURRING theme across this HGB series: even where a conceptually similar IDEA exists under IFRS (fair presentation), HGB's own version is frequently narrower in its MECHANISM (disclosure-only, not override) and is surrounded by ADDITIONAL formal, statutory requirements (language, currency, signature) that IFRS simply leaves to be filled in by whatever national law happens to apply.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§242–245 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>§242 Bilanz/GuV preparation</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">GuV</span></span><span>Together, these form the Jahresabschluss</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>§243(2) clear insight shortfall</span><span><span class="badge be">Anhang</span></span><span>Addressed via additional disclosure, not a change to Bilanz/GuV line items</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>§245 signature</span><span>–</span><span>A formality attaching to the completed Jahresabschluss as a whole, not a specific statement line</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§242–245 connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§§238–241a</span><span>Supply the underlying books and Inventar from which the §242 Bilanz and GuV are constructed</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§§246–256a</span><span>The specific recognition and measurement rules that fill in the general scaffolding §§242–245 establish</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§264(1)–(2)</span><span>Makes §243(3)'s timing standard concrete for corporations, and makes §243(2)'s clear-insight remedy (additional Anhang disclosure) explicit</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§5(1) EStG</span><span>The Maßgeblichkeitsprinzip anchoring tax accounting to the §242–243 commercial balance sheet</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Jahresabschluss</div><div class="glossary-def">The collective term for the Bilanz and GuV together (plus a mandatory Anhang, for corporations).</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Eröffnungsbilanz</div><div class="glossary-def">The opening balance sheet a Kaufmann must prepare at the start of their commercial activity.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Klarer Einblick</div><div class="glossary-def">The "clear insight" requirement – the annual accounts must give a clear view of the enterprise's net assets and results, to the extent the specific provisions allow.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Maßgeblichkeitsprinzip</div><div class="glossary-def">The principle that the commercial (Handelsbilanz) balance sheet is authoritative for the tax (Steuerbilanz) balance sheet, subject to specific tax-law adjustments.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Handelsbilanz</div><div class="glossary-def">The commercial balance sheet prepared under HGB, as distinct from the Steuerbilanz used for tax purposes.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Lagebericht</div><div class="glossary-def">The management report required of Kapitalgesellschaften, kept deliberately outside the Jahresabschluss definition itself.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> Balance sheet
      <span class="badge bp">GuV</span> Income statement (Gewinn- und Verlustrechnung)
      <span class="badge be">Anhang</span> Notes to the financial statements
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
